#
# Build a synthetic GitEHR Store for demos, screenshots, and manual testing.
#
#   s/demo-store                      # build .private/demo-store
#   s/demo-store --output /tmp/demo   # somewhere else
#   s/demo-store --force              # replace an existing demo store
#
# Unlike `s/generate`, which creates bulk repos for benchmarking, this builds
# a small number of clinically coherent records: a multi-year adult record and
# a child record, with typed state, documents, and several contributors.
#
# Everything here is invented. Names are fictional and NHS numbers use the
# 999 range NHS England reserves for test data, so nothing can collide with a
# real patient. Every record's first journal entry says so.
#
# Two behaviours worth knowing before filming:
#
# - Journal entries are backdated through `gitehr import --mode journal`,
#   which preserves each entry's original filename and timestamp, so the
#   timeline really does span years.
# - Typed-state mutations (conditions, medications, ...) write their own
#   journal entries, and those commands have no way to record an entry at the
#   time the event clinically happened - they are stamped "now". A freshly
#   built demo store therefore has ~30 entries dated today sitting on top of
#   the backdated history. The same limitation will apply to importing a real
#   record (roadmap R4), so it is worth fixing rather than working around.

